FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/vm.rs:936 — `Statement::Unless` runs its body as a plain `for stmt in body { execute_statement }` without `run_block`/`execute_statements`, so `break`/`skip` inside `unless` does not stop the rest of that body — the statements after it still run on the tree VM while the bytecode VM (inlined `BREAK`/`SKIP` jump) skips them, diverging — route the body through `run_block` like `Statement::If` at src/vm.rs:929 does.
2. [MAJOR] src/vm.rs:682 — `declare_module` runs the module body via `execute_statement` in a `for stmt in body` loop that only checks `failure`, never `loop_control`, so a `break`/`skip` inside a module body written in a loop leaks the rest of the module body on the tree VM while `BytecodeVm::module` at src/bytecode/vm.rs:2802 sets no `loop_owner` and refuses — run the body through `run_block` and decide whether a module body is inside the caller's loop (like the function-body exemption) so both VMs agree.
3. [MAJOR] src/bytecode/vm.rs:2547 — `discard_frames_above` drops an `object` body abandoned by failure and pops its pending declaration without registering the type, while `Vm::declare_object` at src/vm.rs:1494 registers the type before running the trailing statements, so tree prints the type and bytecode reports `Unknown variable` (FINDINGS §9, still open, no corpus program covers it) — register the abandoned body the way `unwind_frames_above` does for abrupt exits or align both VMs and pin it with a differential test.
REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
